{"id":"c69ef21c-cbbc-487f-b505-8e9a77bb216c","object":"chat.completion","created":1784985759,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # Validate required fields and structure\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must be a dict\\\")\\n    if \\\"nodes\\\" not in case or not isinstance(case[\\\"nodes\\\"], list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must contain 'nodes' list\\\")\\n    if \\\"required_symbols\\\" not in case or not isinstance(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"], list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must contain 'required_symbols' list\\\")\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    req_symbols = set(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n    if not nodes:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"nodes list is empty\\\")\\n    node_map = {}\\n    parent_map = {}\\n    roots = []\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if not isinstance(n, dict):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"each node must be a dict\\\")\\n        for key in (\\\"id\\\", \\\"parent\\\", \\\"symbol\\\", \\\"depth\\\", \\\"removable\\\", \\\"utility\\\"):\\n            if key not in n:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"node missing key: {key}\\\")\\n        nid = n[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if not isinstance(nid, str) or not nid:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"node id must be a non-empty string\\\")\\n        if nid in node_map:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"duplicate node id: {nid}\\\")\\n        node_map[nid] = n\\n        parent = n[\\\"parent\\\"]\\n        if parent is None:\\n            roots.append(nid)\\n        else:\\n            if not isinstance(parent, str) or not parent:\\n                raise ValueError(\\\"parent must be None or a non-empty string\\\")\\n            parent_map[nid] = parent\\n        if not isinstance(n[\\\"symbol\\\"], str):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"symbol must be a string\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(n[\\\"depth\\\"], int) or n[\\\"depth\\\"] < 0:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"depth must be non-negative integer\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(n[\\\"removable\\\"], bool):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"removable must be bool\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(n[\\\"utility\\\"], (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"utility must be numeric\\\")\\n    if len(roots) != 1:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"must have exactly one root (parent=None)\\\")\\n    root_id = roots[0]\\n    # Validate all parent references exist\\n    for nid, pid in parent_map.items():\\n        if pid not in node_map:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"parent '{pid}' not found for node '{nid}'\\\")\\n    # Validate required symbols exist\\n    all_symbols = {n[\\\"symbol\\\"] for n in nodes}\\n    for rs in req_symbols:\\n        if rs not in all_symbols:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"required symbol '{rs}' not present in any node\\\")\\n    # Build initial retained set and symbol counts\\n    retained = set(node for node in node_map)\\n    symbol_count = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        sym = n[\\\"symbol\\\"]\\n        symbol_count[sym] = symbol_count.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n    removed = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if not node[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n        # Never remove root (though root.removable=False, safeguard)\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        # Never remove a node whose symbol is required\\n        if node[\\\"symbol\\\"] in req_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        # Check that node has no retained children (immediate)\\n        has_retained_child = any(\\n            other[\\\"parent\\\"] == nid\\n            for other in nodes\\n            if other[\\\"id\\\"] in retained\\n        )\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        # Check that all required symbols still present after removal\\n        # Since we never remove a node with required symbol, only need to check\\n        # that removal does not cause any required symbol count to become zero\\n        # (This is redundant but safe)\\n        sym = node[\\\"symbol\\\"]\\n        if sym in req_symbols:\\n            # Should not happen because we already skipped\\n            continue\\n        # Actually, removal of this node does not affect required symbols\\n        # because node's symbol is not required. The count of required symbols\\n        # remains unchanged. So we can proceed.\\n        # Remove node\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        symbol_count[sym] -= 1\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n    # Sort retained node ids\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function `solve(case)` that implements a reduction algorithm on parse trees. The case contains a list of nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols. The goal: given a tree, remove nodes (if removable) while ensuring that all required symbols remain present in the tree (i.e., at least one node with that symbol remains). Root is not removable. The algorithm should be: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we need to process nodes in decreasing depth order, and within same depth, stable tie break by node ID (string). For each node that is removable, tentatively remove it, but only if after removal all required symbols are still present (i.e., there is at least one node with each required symbol in the remaining tree). Also, never remove a node with retained children? The description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So before removal, we must check that the node has no children that are still in the tree (i.e., all descendants must already be removed). Actually \"node with retained children\" means if any of its children (descendants) are still in the retained set, we cannot remove it. So we need to process bottom-up: deeper nodes first, so children are considered before parents. So we can simply iterate nodes sorted by depth descending, and then by ID ascending. For each node, if it is removable and its symbol is not required (but required_symbols list includes symbols, not node IDs), and if all its children (immediate or all descendants? It says \"retained children\" - likely immediate children that are still retained. Since we process bottom-up, by the time we consider a parent, its children would have been processed. So we can check if any child of this node (by parent field) is still in the retained set. If none, we can consider removal. Also check that after removing this node, each required symbol is still present. That means that there must be some node with that symbol in the retained set (including the node itself before removal). After removal, we need to ensure that for each required symbol, there is at least one node in the retained set (excluding the current node). Since the node itself might be the only node with its symbol, we must not remove it if that symbol is required. Also, note that the node might have a symbol that is required, but there could be other nodes with the same symbol? The case shows nodes have same symbols. So we need to count symbols. So algorithm:\n\n1. Build a dict mapping node_id -> node object.\n2. Build a list of all node_ids (initially all retained). We'll maintain a set of retained node ids.\n3. Determine set of required_symbols.\n4. Create a mapping from symbol to count of nodes with that symbol in retained set. Initially count all symbols.\n5. Sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending.\n6. For each node in that order:\n   - If node is not removable: skip.\n   - If node.id is root? Root is not removable per field, but also check if symbol in required_symbols: then skip because required symbol nodes should not be removed (as per instruction: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So for any node with symbol in required_symbols, skip. But careful: The instruction says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we skip if node's symbol is in required_symbols.\n   - Check if node has any retained children: can check if any other node in retained set has parent == node.id. Since we process bottom-up, children might already be removed. If any child retained, skip.\n   - Check if after removal, all required symbols still have count >=1. That is, for each sym in required_symbols, if sym == node.symbol, then count after removal would be count[sym] - 1. If that becomes 0, skip removal.\n   - If all conditions satisfied, remove the node: remove from retained set, decrement symbol count. Record removed node id in removal order (order of removal as we go). Record the node id in a list removed_node_ids.\n   - Continue.\n\n7. After processing all nodes, the retained node ids are those still in set. Sort retained_node_ids (alphabetically) as per example? The output requires \"sorted retained IDs\" and \"the actual removal order\". So we need to output removed_node_ids in the order they were removed. retained_node_ids sorted (presumably alphabetically). The output schema expects arrays of strings.\n\nEdge Cases: If a node has children that are retained but those children are not removable? That's fine, we cannot remove the parent. But children that are not removable will remain, preventing removal.\n\nNote: The case may also include nodes with \"removable\": false. Those are never removed.\n\nAlso, need to ensure that we never remove a node that would cause a required symbol to disappear. So we check counts.\n\nInput `case` is the payload object. It has \"nodes\" list and \"required_symbols\" list. Also maybe \"_case_nonce\" but ignore.\n\nReturn JSON object with \"removed_node_ids\" (list in removal order) and \"retained_node_ids\" (sorted list).\n\nImplementation steps in Python:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    req_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    # Build maps\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    retained = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    symbol_count = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        sym = n[\"symbol\"]\n        symbol_count[sym] = symbol_count.get(sym, 0) + 1\n\n    # sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n\n    removed = []\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        # skip root? but root has removable false so not needed\n        # skip required symbol\n        if node[\"symbol\"] in req_symbols:\n            continue\n        # check retained children\n        has_retained_child = any(\n            other[\"parent\"] == nid for other in nodes if other[\"id\"] in retained\n        )\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # check required symbols after removal\n        # only need to check if this node's symbol is the only one with that symbol\n        sym = node[\"symbol\"]\n        if sym in req_symbols:\n            # but we already skipped required symbols, so this won't happen\n            continue\n        # also check that no required symbol becomes zero; but since node's symbol not required, only need to check required symbols that are currently present.\n        # For required symbols, they are already present, and removal of this node doesn't affect them because it's not that symbol.\n        # However if required symbol exists only in this node? But node's symbol not required, so it doesn't matter.\n        # But consider that required symbols might be present in multiple nodes. Removal doesn't affect.\n        # So no further check needed? Actually what if the node has a symbol that is not required, but its removal could affect the count of another required symbol? No, that's independent.\n        # So only need to ensure that for each required symbol, count > 0. Since we are not removing nodes with required symbol, counts remain.\n        # However, there is nuance: what if this node is the only node that provides a symbol that is not required? That's fine. So we can proceed.\n        # But wait: The instruction says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That means after removal, check that each required symbol still has at least one node. Since we never remove a node with required symbol, it's automatically satisfied. However, consider scenario where a required symbol's only node is removable? But we skip nodes with required symbol, so that's not possible. So we can skip that check. But to be safe, we can still perform the check.\n        # Let's do the check for completeness:\n        if any(symbol_count[rs] == 0 for rs in req_symbols if rs in symbol_count):\n            # should not happen initially\n            continue\n        # Remove node\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        symbol_count[sym] -= 1\n        removed.append(nid)\n    # Sort retained IDs\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n\nBut need to ensure that we handle cases where symbol_count may not have a required symbol? The required symbols should be present in the tree (given). The field \"required_symbols\" list symbols that must be present in the final tree. So initially they are present.\n\nAlso need to check if any node has parent null? Only root.\n\nPotential issue: \"node with retained children\" - does that mean immediate children or any descendant? Since we process bottom-up, immediate children suffice. Because if a child is removed, then parent's immediate children lost. But if a descendant is retained but not immediate child, the immediate child might be removed, then the parent will have no direct retained children? Actually if a parent node has a child that is removed, but that child itself had a retained descendant, that descendant is not a direct child of the parent. So the parent would have no retained direct children, but it would still have a retained descendant. Should we forbid removal in that case? The phrase \"node with retained children\" likely means any retained descendant. Usually in tree reduction, you cannot remove a node if any of its descendants are still in the tree. Since we process depth-first bottom-up, we would have already processed descendants. If a descendant is retained, that means it wasn't removed earlier because it might be not removable or required. So its parent, when considered later, would have a retained descendant. But if the parent's immediate child is removed, the descendant is not a direct child. So we need to check any descendant, not just immediate children. To be safe, we should check that no node in the retained set has an ancestor chain that goes through this node. That is, for each retained node, check if its parent chain includes this node id. That could be expensive. Alternatively, we can process by bottom-up and at each node, we only remove it if all its children are already removed (i.e., no child in retained set). But that may not capture deeper descendants if there is a chain where a child is removed but its child (the descendant) is retained? That's impossible because if a child is removed, all its descendants should have been considered earlier (they are deeper). Since we process deeper nodes first, by the time we remove a child, we would have processed all its descendants. If any descendant is retained, then the child would not be removable because it would have a retained child (the descendant). So if the child is removed, it means all its descendants were removed. Therefore, when we reach a parent, if any child is still in retained set, that child has no retained descendants (because it would have been processed too). So checking immediate children is sufficient because if a child is retained, it means all its descendants are also retained? No, if a child is retained, it could have had descendants that were removed. So the child is retained but its descendants may be removed. That's fine. But then the parent has a retained child (the child node itself), so it cannot be removed. So we only need to check immediate children. Because if a descendant is retained but the immediate child is removed, that means the descendant's parent (the immediate child) is removed, but the descendant is still retained. That seems contradictory: if a node is removed, all its descendants should also be removed because we process bottom-up and we remove a node only after its children are removed. So if the descendant is retained, its parent (immediate child) would have been processed earlier. Since descendant is deeper, it would be considered before its parent. If the descendant is retained, then when its parent (immediate child) is considered, it would have a retained child (the descendant), so the parent would not be removed. Therefore, it's impossible for the immediate child to be removed while the descendant is retained. So it's consistent: retained nodes form a connected forest? Actually removal preserves connectivity? Not necessarily, but the condition \"never remove a node with retained children\" ensures that we only remove leaf nodes (or nodes whose children are already removed). So immediate children check is sufficient.\n\nThus the algorithm stands.\n\nNow we need to implement and output as JSON with key \"implementation\" and value being the source code string. Need to escape appropriately. Also note that the function name must be `solve` and takes argument `case`.\n\nWe also need to handle potential edge cases: if there is a required symbol that is only present on a removable node? But we skip removable nodes with required symbols, so that's fine. If initial tree does not contain a required symbol, that would be invalid, but the instruction says raise ValueError. So we should validate that all required symbols are present initially. Also check that node list is non-empty, root exists, etc. But the task says \"Validate every required field before computing a result\" and \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So we need to add validation.\n\nLet's add validation:\n\n- case must have \"nodes\" (list) and \"required_symbols\" (list). Raise ValueError if missing or wrong type.\n- For each node: must have \"id\" (string non-empty), \"parent\" (string or None for root), \"symbol\" (string), \"depth\" (int non-negative), \"removable\" (bool), \"utility\" (float). Raise ValueError if missing or wrong type.\n- There must be exactly one root node (parent None). If multiple roots or none, raise ValueError.\n- All parent references must refer to existing node ids. If not, raise ValueError.\n- All required_symbols must be present in at least one node's symbol. If not, raise ValueError.\n- No duplicate node ids.\n\nWe can implement these checks.\n\nAlso note that the output requires \"retained_node_ids\" sorted. We'll sort them.\n\nNow produce final answer as JSON with \"implementation\" key.\n\nMake sure to escape backslashes, quotes, newlines in the source string.\n\nI'll write the source code as a single string with triple quotes? Actually in JSON, we need to escape. I'll write the Python code as a string with proper escaping.\n\nLet's construct the full solve function with validation and algorithm.\n\nI'll output the JSON now."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":4276,"total_tokens":6448,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":